WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.351 --> 00:00:02.790
- Good morning Believe
Nation, today's message is

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:04.691
take your shot!

00:00:04.691 --> 00:00:06.441
Over to you Henry Rollins.

00:00:06.441 --> 00:00:09.049
(rooster crowing)

00:00:09.049 --> 00:00:13.216
♫ I wake up every morning,
espresso keep me going.

00:00:14.327 --> 00:00:18.437
♫ I wake up every morning.

00:00:18.437 --> 00:00:20.858
- The last straight job I had was 1981.

00:00:20.858 --> 00:00:23.576
Head manager of a Haagen
Dazs in Georgetown,

00:00:23.576 --> 00:00:26.007
the Washington DC on Wisconsin
Avenue at the intersection

00:00:26.007 --> 00:00:29.458
of Wisconsin and O Street
making $3.75 an hour

00:00:29.458 --> 00:00:31.236
or $4 an hour something like that.

00:00:31.236 --> 00:00:33.721
Working 40 to 60 hours a week.

00:00:33.721 --> 00:00:35.220
I had a small apartment which I shared

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:36.509
with an old pal of mine.

00:00:36.509 --> 00:00:39.521
I had a small record collection,

00:00:39.521 --> 00:00:41.688
an ailing V.W. automobile,

00:00:44.563 --> 00:00:47.066
and a little tiny life with my

00:00:47.066 --> 00:00:49.563
minimum wage job which I liked.

00:00:49.563 --> 00:00:52.424
And I looked at my life and
realized this is probably

00:00:52.424 --> 00:00:54.223
about as good as it's going to get for me.

00:00:54.223 --> 00:00:56.293
I might quit this place
and go work at my friend's

00:00:56.293 --> 00:00:58.180
record store, cause I
liked being around records

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:00.284
that I could probably
run his store very well,

00:01:00.284 --> 00:01:02.974
knowing what I knew about
retail at that point.

00:01:02.974 --> 00:01:05.206
And then I got a very lucky break.

00:01:05.206 --> 00:01:06.903
Cause at that same time
I was very frustrated.

00:01:06.903 --> 00:01:09.935
I'd say well, this is going
to be a very tough life.

00:01:09.935 --> 00:01:12.695
It's going to be a life of a
lot of standing on my feet,

00:01:12.695 --> 00:01:15.377
a lot of work, taking
it from other people,

00:01:15.377 --> 00:01:19.544
and having very little to
show for it by Friday evening.

00:01:21.926 --> 00:01:26.093
Then Black Flag, the famous
band who is friends of mine,

00:01:27.813 --> 00:01:30.631
they played in New York.
I was in Washington.

00:01:30.631 --> 00:01:33.054
I took a ride up to go see them cause

00:01:33.054 --> 00:01:34.644
they weren't coming down to my town.

00:01:34.644 --> 00:01:38.823
I jumped on stage and sang
with them that night a song.

00:01:38.823 --> 00:01:40.144
Cause I had to go back down to DC

00:01:40.144 --> 00:01:42.121
and go work some awful shift.

00:01:42.121 --> 00:01:44.800
They called me a few days
later at the ice cream store

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.494
and said we're looking for
a singer cause the rhythm

00:01:47.494 --> 00:01:49.816
guitar player wants to, I'm
sorry the vocalist wants to

00:01:49.816 --> 00:01:52.244
move down to rhythm guitar.

00:01:52.244 --> 00:01:54.053
You can tell I've told this story before.

00:01:54.053 --> 00:01:56.445
And we're holding auditions,
do you want a crack at this?

00:01:56.445 --> 00:01:57.644
Cause we saw you on stage the other night,

00:01:57.644 --> 00:01:58.993
you're pretty wild.

00:01:58.993 --> 00:02:01.066
I looked at the ice cream scoop in my hand

00:02:01.066 --> 00:02:05.233
my chocolate spattered apron,
and my future in the world

00:02:06.595 --> 00:02:08.955
of minimum wage work, or
I could go up to New York

00:02:08.955 --> 00:02:11.555
and audition for this crazy
band who is my favorite.

00:02:11.555 --> 00:02:13.305
What's the worse that's
going to happen to me?

00:02:13.305 --> 00:02:15.935
I miss a day of work.
Ooh, there goes 21 bucks.

00:02:15.935 --> 00:02:20.245
And I get humiliated in
front of my favorite band.

00:02:20.245 --> 00:02:24.713
Humiliation and young
people kind of go together.

00:02:24.713 --> 00:02:26.230
I was use to it.

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:30.146
So I took a train up there,
I walked into this practice

00:02:30.146 --> 00:02:31.875
place in the East Village.

00:02:31.875 --> 00:02:33.283
I'm standing there with the band

00:02:33.283 --> 00:02:34.682
with a microphone in my hand.

00:02:34.682 --> 00:02:36.034
They said pick the tune.

00:02:36.034 --> 00:02:39.923
I sang every song they had,
improvising most of it,

00:02:39.923 --> 00:02:41.944
two times through, we did the set.

00:02:41.944 --> 00:02:43.665
They said okay, you have a seat.

00:02:43.665 --> 00:02:45.881
We're going to go have a band
meeting. Whatever that means.

00:02:45.881 --> 00:02:47.993
And they came back like ten minutes later

00:02:47.993 --> 00:02:49.224
and they said okay, you're in.

00:02:49.224 --> 00:02:50.132
I said what do you mean? They said

00:02:50.132 --> 00:02:51.456
you're the singer in Black Flag.

00:02:51.456 --> 00:02:53.006
I said so what do I do?

00:02:53.006 --> 00:02:55.554
They go, you quit your
job, you pack your gear,

00:02:55.554 --> 00:02:59.104
you meet us on the road.
Here's the tour itinerary,

00:02:59.104 --> 00:03:01.194
here's the lyrics. We'll see you soon.

00:03:01.194 --> 00:03:05.455
So fairly numb, I went back
down to Washington with

00:03:05.455 --> 00:03:07.432
this passel of lyrics in my hand.

00:03:07.432 --> 00:03:10.704
Went to my boss and said
I'm not exactly quitting,

00:03:10.704 --> 00:03:13.064
but here's this thing that happened.

00:03:13.064 --> 00:03:15.535
He said, it's your shot, I said yeah.

00:03:15.535 --> 00:03:16.864
He said, take it.

00:03:16.864 --> 00:03:19.465
- When I was 20 years old,
I missed my shot because

00:03:19.465 --> 00:03:22.225
I was scared and I vowed
never to do it again.

00:03:22.225 --> 00:03:25.845
I was in Paris, I was on
vacation, I was in University.

00:03:25.845 --> 00:03:29.055
I'm sitting here with my map,

00:03:29.055 --> 00:03:31.385
outside the Notre Dame Cathedral in Paris.

00:03:31.385 --> 00:03:33.725
And a girl comes up and
asks me for directions.

00:03:33.725 --> 00:03:35.203
But she was a French girl.

00:03:35.203 --> 00:03:38.415
And it didn't twig until later, that

00:03:38.415 --> 00:03:42.676
why would a French girl be
asking me for directions

00:03:42.676 --> 00:03:46.812
in English, didn't
really make sense right?

00:03:46.812 --> 00:03:49.372
And then I thought, you know
what I should ask her out.

00:03:49.372 --> 00:03:51.056
Just see what she's doing
tonight, should take her to,

00:03:51.056 --> 00:03:53.654
ask her out for coffee
or dinner or something.

00:03:53.654 --> 00:03:54.936
See what she's up to.

00:03:54.936 --> 00:03:57.035
But I was afraid. I was afraid to do it.

00:03:57.035 --> 00:04:00.494
I let her walk away, because really

00:04:00.494 --> 00:04:02.076
I was afraid of rejection.

00:04:02.076 --> 00:04:04.387
As she's walking away, the
Notre Dame is on a river,

00:04:04.387 --> 00:04:06.424
and I'm on one side of the
river, and she crosses over

00:04:06.424 --> 00:04:08.245
to the other side of the river.

00:04:08.245 --> 00:04:12.412
I was so upset at myself
that I didn't' take my shot.

00:04:13.326 --> 00:04:15.243
I was so upset that I didn't try.

00:04:15.243 --> 00:04:16.815
At least try.

00:04:16.815 --> 00:04:18.812
What I did was I ended
up taking a picture.

00:04:18.812 --> 00:04:21.555
As I took the picture,
she actually walked behind

00:04:21.555 --> 00:04:24.536
one of the market stalls,
and I didn't actually get her

00:04:24.536 --> 00:04:26.817
picture, it's fine. So I
have this beautiful picture

00:04:26.817 --> 00:04:28.875
of this French riverside.

00:04:28.875 --> 00:04:31.744
What I did when I got home,
I was still upset about it.

00:04:31.744 --> 00:04:34.355
I blew the picture up,
and I put it on my wall.

00:04:34.355 --> 00:04:37.215
I was actually trying to find
the picture to show you guys.

00:04:37.215 --> 00:04:39.486
But I couldn't find it
anywhere in my files.

00:04:39.486 --> 00:04:41.176
I blew it up though and
I put it on my wall,

00:04:41.176 --> 00:04:45.705
so I'm at University,
everybody who walks in asks me

00:04:45.705 --> 00:04:48.454
about the picture, and
it just looks like a nice

00:04:48.454 --> 00:04:50.949
beautiful French scene.

00:04:50.949 --> 00:04:53.567
But I didn't' tell anybody what
it really represented to me.

00:04:53.567 --> 00:04:54.998
Why it was really so important.

00:04:54.998 --> 00:04:58.109
And I looked at that picture
everyday, because the next time

00:04:58.109 --> 00:05:01.226
that I had to take my shot I
wanted to go out and do it.

00:05:01.226 --> 00:05:04.626
I didn't want to be afraid,
I wanted to conquer my fear

00:05:04.626 --> 00:05:08.786
and say yes. And this
served me really well

00:05:08.786 --> 00:05:12.717
A year or so later when I
had to make the really tough

00:05:12.717 --> 00:05:17.516
decision, do I take this
job that I always wanted.

00:05:17.516 --> 00:05:19.309
That had these high
paying job opportunities

00:05:19.309 --> 00:05:21.877
80-100 thousand dollar
starting salary with a company

00:05:21.877 --> 00:05:23.509
that I really wanted to go at.

00:05:23.509 --> 00:05:25.917
Or continue with my start
up that was failing,

00:05:25.917 --> 00:05:27.476
that was making $300 a month.

00:05:27.476 --> 00:05:29.909
The logical, practical,
safe, easy thing to do

00:05:29.909 --> 00:05:31.258
would have been to take the job.

00:05:31.258 --> 00:05:32.268
It's what I thought I always wanted,

00:05:32.268 --> 00:05:34.254
it's what my friends wanted,
it's what you're suppose to do

00:05:34.254 --> 00:05:35.749
coming out of University.

00:05:35.749 --> 00:05:38.268
I didn't want to live with
the regret of not knowing

00:05:38.268 --> 00:05:39.789
what happened with my business.

00:05:39.789 --> 00:05:43.872
Looking at that picture
daily reminded me to take

00:05:45.417 --> 00:05:48.569
my shot the next time it comes around.

00:05:48.569 --> 00:05:52.897
I gave myself a year to say, I'm
going to push off those jobs.

00:05:52.897 --> 00:05:54.798
They'll still be there in a year.

00:05:54.798 --> 00:05:56.948
Even if it's not those
jobs, it's some other job.

00:05:56.948 --> 00:06:00.919
I'm going to give this a shot.
I can deal with the failure.

00:06:00.919 --> 00:06:03.316
I can deal with the rejection.

00:06:03.316 --> 00:06:06.498
I can deal with it not working
out, even though that's

00:06:06.498 --> 00:06:09.418
really kind of short term intense pain.

00:06:09.418 --> 00:06:14.227
What I can't deal with is
the regret of not knowing

00:06:14.227 --> 00:06:16.218
because that's not some short term

00:06:16.218 --> 00:06:18.618
minimal pain, that's forever.

00:06:18.618 --> 00:06:23.166
If I chose not to focus on my
business, and I took that job.

00:06:23.166 --> 00:06:27.333
I would forever look back
on that moment and think

00:06:29.628 --> 00:06:34.256
what if? What if I just
tried a little bit harder?

00:06:34.256 --> 00:06:36.039
What if I gave it a little bit more?

00:06:36.039 --> 00:06:37.398
Yeah you know but it wasn't working,

00:06:37.398 --> 00:06:38.307
it wasn't getting results.

00:06:38.307 --> 00:06:40.198
But what if I gave it a little bit more?

00:06:40.198 --> 00:06:42.529
I did not want to live with that regret.

00:06:42.529 --> 00:06:44.697
I'm sure you've had
moments in your life too,

00:06:44.697 --> 00:06:45.577
where you didn't take the shot.

00:06:45.577 --> 00:06:50.022
Where you had an opportunity
right in front of you

00:06:50.022 --> 00:06:51.318
and you didn't go for it.

00:06:51.318 --> 00:06:55.709
Like Eminem says, if you had
one shot, one opportunity

00:06:55.709 --> 00:06:59.876
to seize everything you ever
wanted, would you take it?

00:07:00.768 --> 00:07:04.768
That picture on the wall
helped me take my shot.

00:07:04.768 --> 00:07:09.369
I hope sincerely that the
next time you get your chance

00:07:09.369 --> 00:07:12.248
at something, that you don't shrink down,

00:07:12.248 --> 00:07:14.136
that you don't say no out of fear.

00:07:14.136 --> 00:07:16.059
That you don't say no
because you find some

00:07:16.059 --> 00:07:18.079
practical reason why you can't do it.

00:07:18.079 --> 00:07:19.788
That you take your shot, and if you've

00:07:19.788 --> 00:07:22.621
had those moments that you regret,

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:26.698
where you wanted to take
your shot and you didn't.

00:07:26.698 --> 00:07:30.052
Give yourself a visual
reminder like I did in my room.

00:07:30.052 --> 00:07:32.969
To say this is not happening again.

00:07:34.391 --> 00:07:37.224
Next time, I'm going to take my shot.

00:07:39.581 --> 00:07:41.150
I hope you take yours.

00:07:41.150 --> 00:07:43.371
So the question of the
day today is I'm curious;

00:07:43.371 --> 00:07:45.371
What is a shot you didn't take?

00:07:45.371 --> 00:07:47.704
What's your biggest regret in life or

00:07:47.704 --> 00:07:49.972
business for you right now?

00:07:49.972 --> 00:07:52.264
Write it down, make it painful,

00:07:52.264 --> 00:07:55.356
go back into that terrible memory.

00:07:55.356 --> 00:07:58.234
Cause I never want that
to happen to you again.

00:07:58.234 --> 00:08:00.325
So write it down, leave
it in the comments.

00:08:00.325 --> 00:08:02.066
I'm looking forward to seeing
what you guys put down.

00:08:02.066 --> 00:08:04.631
Finally I want to give a quick
shout out to Ingrid Prescott.

00:08:04.631 --> 00:08:06.962
Ingrid thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book.

00:08:06.962 --> 00:08:10.013
"Your One Word," I really
appreciate your support.

00:08:10.013 --> 00:08:11.886
Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:08:11.886 --> 00:08:13.647
I believe in you, I hope
you continue to believe

00:08:13.647 --> 00:08:16.797
in yourself, and whatever
your one word is,

00:08:16.797 --> 00:08:18.747
and I'll see you again
tomorrow morning for another

00:08:18.747 --> 00:08:20.164
shot of espresso.

00:08:21.015 --> 00:08:25.182
♫ I wake up every morning,
espresso keep me going.

00:08:27.696 --> 00:08:29.335
Hey Believe Nation, thank
you so much for watching.

00:08:29.335 --> 00:08:31.356
If you want to learn
more about this subject,

00:08:31.356 --> 00:08:32.984
get a little bit deeper on it,

00:08:32.984 --> 00:08:34.615
I'm going to share some extra bonus clips

00:08:34.615 --> 00:08:36.606
that I hope you'll enjoy.

00:08:36.606 --> 00:08:39.606
- After you moved to LA from Hawaii,

00:08:41.547 --> 00:08:43.497
you got there and what's the reason

00:08:43.497 --> 00:08:46.077
to leave Hawaii in the first place?

00:08:46.077 --> 00:08:49.116
- I think I needed to take a shot

00:08:49.116 --> 00:08:50.944
at the big time.

00:08:50.944 --> 00:08:53.111
I wanted to make my album,

00:08:54.264 --> 00:08:56.514
and start writing my own music,

00:08:56.514 --> 00:08:59.064
I've been performing my
whole life in Hawaii,

00:08:59.064 --> 00:09:02.697
in Waikiki and parties,
wedding, and shows.

00:09:02.697 --> 00:09:04.704
Anything you could think of.

00:09:04.704 --> 00:09:07.085
Restaurants, bars, and clubs.

00:09:07.085 --> 00:09:10.633
I feel like I did a lot
in Hawaii and I wanted to,

00:09:10.633 --> 00:09:12.914
as soon as I graduated
high school, I wanted to

00:09:12.914 --> 00:09:17.081
take the next step and
hopefully do it even bigger.

00:09:18.357 --> 00:09:21.160
- You got there to LA with just

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:23.077
your suitcase probably?

00:09:23.923 --> 00:09:25.443
- Wasn't even my suitcase.

00:09:25.443 --> 00:09:26.281
- Any money?

00:09:26.281 --> 00:09:27.854
- Absolutely not.

00:09:27.854 --> 00:09:30.413
I probably had about
600 bucks I'd saved up.

00:09:30.413 --> 00:09:31.246
700 maybe.

00:09:32.494 --> 00:09:36.756
- What was the plan then?
How did you feel at this

00:09:36.756 --> 00:09:39.545
special moment getting there and having

00:09:39.545 --> 00:09:42.616
no clue what's going to happen next?

00:09:42.616 --> 00:09:46.830
- It was exciting, I was
young, my sister helped me out.

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:49.233
She let me stay with her for a year

00:09:49.233 --> 00:09:52.150
and I got signed to Motown records.

00:09:53.314 --> 00:09:55.291
That didn't really work
out, but I had to learn.

00:09:55.291 --> 00:09:59.458
It's such a learning process
and a lot of rejection.

00:10:00.460 --> 00:10:03.752
I think people have to
understand that it's just a part

00:10:03.752 --> 00:10:07.919
of what it takes to finally
come up with your own album.

00:10:11.318 --> 00:10:13.678
You need to be rejected over and over.

00:10:13.678 --> 00:10:15.109
And everyone telling you
you're not good enough,

00:10:15.109 --> 00:10:19.627
to kind of break though
that, that's what it takes.

00:10:19.627 --> 00:10:23.436
- You sent unfinished
work to Stephen Sondheim

00:10:23.436 --> 00:10:24.707
for him to critique.

00:10:24.707 --> 00:10:28.249
- Well it was pretty well
(mumbles) by the time I sent it.

00:10:28.249 --> 00:10:30.458
- So Steve, just tell me what you think.

00:10:30.458 --> 00:10:32.216
How does that work, and are you really ...

00:10:32.216 --> 00:10:33.549
- Yeah, it's ...

00:10:38.404 --> 00:10:42.571
It's a crazy idea. But in my
head it was a really good idea.

00:10:43.978 --> 00:10:46.709
Sometimes what you need are people

00:10:46.709 --> 00:10:50.360
to just not say that's a terrible idea.

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:53.039
You have to be very careful
about who you let in

00:10:53.039 --> 00:10:55.161
when you've had a good
idea, because people

00:10:55.161 --> 00:10:56.249
can crush it for you.

00:10:56.249 --> 00:10:58.429
People can go no, I would
stick to this other thing.

00:10:58.429 --> 00:11:01.169
If you trust that person,
and you love that person,

00:11:01.169 --> 00:11:04.307
it can really affect your
own view of your work.

00:11:04.307 --> 00:11:05.761
I learned that the hard way,

00:11:05.761 --> 00:11:07.250
I've had ideas die in front of me.

00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:11.417
Because people I respect go,
maybe not that one so much.

00:11:12.627 --> 00:11:14.070
When I was on vacation and I picked up

00:11:14.070 --> 00:11:16.761
Chirinos' book and I started reading it

00:11:16.761 --> 00:11:18.079
and reading it to my wife and being like,

00:11:18.079 --> 00:11:19.170
there's something here.

00:11:19.170 --> 00:11:21.649
Being like I'm not a crazy person.

00:11:21.649 --> 00:11:24.428
My wife was like no
that's a really cool idea.

00:11:24.428 --> 00:11:26.931
That's as responsible for this as anything

00:11:26.931 --> 00:11:29.549
I remember when I first met Sondheim,

00:11:29.549 --> 00:11:30.960
it was to do translations for the

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:32.899
last revival of West Side Story.

00:11:32.899 --> 00:11:37.459
We talked shop, and we talked
about what should translate,

00:11:37.459 --> 00:11:39.131
and what I shouldn't, and
we kind of talked about

00:11:39.131 --> 00:11:40.599
that for about a half an hour.

00:11:40.599 --> 00:11:43.130
Then he asked me about what
are you thinking about next.

00:11:43.130 --> 00:11:45.989
Heights was running at the
time, it was it's first year.

00:11:45.989 --> 00:11:49.789
I said, I think I have this
idea, Alexander Hamilton.

00:11:49.789 --> 00:11:53.730
He threw up his head and
made this, and he's a really

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:57.838
reserved guy, and he went that
is the most incredible idea.

00:11:57.838 --> 00:12:01.338
I just instantly got up and I was like ...

00:12:05.912 --> 00:12:08.719
That's also as responsible
for us being here as anything.

00:12:08.719 --> 00:12:13.018
That early encouragement, his whole thing.

